#{[{:activity/expected-seconds 600.0 :activity/first-step {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111702} :activity/id #uuid "33a94a7b-379f-5b7b-bcb4-80cdb16140fb" :activity/scope {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186048789 :topic/id #uuid "bf1964cf-beba-5911-abd1-76470578e4aa" :topic/math-academy-id 1530} :activity/steps [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111702 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111825 :tutorial/content "Suppose we wish to plot the graph of $y=2^x.$ First, we create a table of values:\n\n| $x$ | $-3$ | $-2$ | $-1$ | $0$ | $1$ | $2$ | $3$ |\n| --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- | --- |\n| $y = 2^x$ | $\\dfrac18$ | $\\dfrac14$ | $\\dfrac12$ | $1$ | $2$ | $4$ | $8$ |\n\nNow, we can plot the graph:\n\n![](images/e9/e987a8647092c836bde4bcea31dc075e80e9da36a67133fc2b9431b99c5e42ed.png)\n\nNote the following key points:\n\n1\\. The graph has a $y$-intercept at $(0,1).$\n\n2\\. The graph is increasing.\n\n3\\. For $x>0,$ the graph grows very rapidly as $x$ increases. Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow \\infty$ as $x\\rightarrow \\infty.$\n\n4\\. For $x<0,$ the graph gets very close to zero as $x$ decreases. However, it never actually reaches zero, so the graph has the horizontal asymptote $y=0.$ Symbolically, we write $y\\rightarrow 0$ as $x\\rightarrow -\\infty.$\n\n5\\. The domain is $x\\in (-\\infty , \\infty )$, and the range is $y>0.$\n\nThese key points apply to *any* exponential curve $y=a^x$ with $a>1.$ These graphs always have the same general shape:\n\n![](images/48/48f7bc7bb25bf1955d1e2cf48cffbf09e17f80a1228cada1c31f7ad4822bdace.png)" :tutorial/id #uuid "1a4d886d-d7b8-5667-b52a-2c2eb4a35b91" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1540 :tutorial/title "Introduction"} :step/id #uuid "af86e00c-f654-5321-9355-dbe234a657e3" :step/math-academy-id 516446 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111703}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111703 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110732 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110310 :question/id #uuid "99ab6141-164f-5f36-b55b-6e7fa1c8770c" :question/math-academy-id "e-1215"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "cf40e17e-0461-54a3-8f23-8424d694f189" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186049619}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111145} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111335} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508046} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508090}] :knowledge-point/title "Sketching the Graph of an Exponential Growth Function"} :step/id #uuid "9c436809-f5cb-527a-907e-bd891aa0951c" :step/math-academy-id 516447 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111704}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111704 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110733 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110311 :question/id #uuid "45473f44-206e-5eb6-a663-04d264e6a890" :question/math-academy-id "e-1216"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "f6fa9e7b-172d-57b6-8b6e-0db0f8f802ad" :knowledge-point/key-prerequisites [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050255} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186050580}] :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111223} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111334} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508053} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186508080} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186570744}] :knowledge-point/title "Identifying True Statements about Exponential Growth Functions"} :step/id #uuid "de707c0a-c932-5251-81f5-798ae37e792e" :step/math-academy-id 516450 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111705}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111705 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111826 :tutorial/content "Multiplying an exponential growth function by a positive number **scales** the graph in the vertical direction.\n\nFor example, the curve ![](images/68/68dcb337cb0927d43403eebeb21c0a54dc46a136f7cd21ce821d9bfc325c748e.svg) represents a vertical scaling of ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) by a factor of ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg)  It takes all of the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-coordinates on ![](images/76/763bdfd3547d86e0c619eb55c57d6d3b83c3405c6fa04c01f4405a056475e9cf.svg) and multiplies them by ![](images/27/270d33581e0657a10d567c6cfaa529521b34e20a39c9a29550698cbf252fabfa.svg) This has the effect of **stretching** the curve upwards.\n\n![](images/70/7052037576cfaf29807f0ad332100b9929a72806846219da2599c8f911500d87.png)\n\nNotice that the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remained the same, and the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moved from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/43/437619dfcf8db5be350de504dc9d98eab13c2ac21c62998d3c268a1f475a30d9.svg)\n\nIn general, for *any* vertically stretched exponential curve ![](images/8c/8ccfcf186ac190ccb8f91676958e6030b4224bfd46b3598261736fbfcb7c351f.svg)\n\n- the horizontal asymptote at ![](images/b3/b3259a74b1b63f2b49841acd0eea4f599e9dacf9c4f1b17bda0a3cb115a9d90e.svg) remains the same, and\n\n- the ![](images/1d/1d9b846cface9f9348e4081a0dd87dd254bf26663c12aa0d1a15aa24d2393261.svg)-intercept moves from ![](images/42/42f562f132f08838fe095cdeb883c38dd65e0d77828f65f73d783980c52f34ec.svg) to ![](images/e8/e8c4c8598a8228afb25cbf13c034e2fe234393f9b9c0dfde8be1726b4fc79a8e.svg)" :tutorial/id #uuid "8c1fa510-e5f3-585b-852a-c46724144ec1" :tutorial/math-academy-id 1541 :tutorial/title "Vertical Stretching"} :step/id #uuid "82779645-e04b-59c6-a05e-c042063885a7" :step/math-academy-id 516453 :step/next {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111706}} {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111706 :step/content {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110734 :knowledge-point/canonical-example {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186110369 :question/id #uuid "e5680653-d529-58df-b243-7447db0a750a" :question/math-academy-id "e-1802"} :knowledge-point/id #uuid "c7654997-ee9a-5d6c-88e9-d46f36bd8e88" :knowledge-point/questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186111098} {:db/id #edb/ref 